00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.390
I'm gonna challenge you
to take a non-crispy dish

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.530
and make it crispy.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.820
- All right, Owen. What'd you bring me?

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.260
- So here we have a classic tiramisu.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.060
This is my favorite dish.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.670
I'm not a sweets guy, but
if I see this on the menu

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.170
I'm getting it every time.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.710
Tiramisu literally
translates to pick me up.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.630
So it's like a dish that's supposed to,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:52.620
you know, make you feel good.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.100
It's light, it's got coffee, mascarpone,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.280
but the thing it's lacking is texture,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.113
and that's where you come in.

00:01:00.113 --> 00:01:01.230
You think you can do it?

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.020
- I think I got a few tricks,
but we'll see what I can do.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.640
Let's break down the tiramisu.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.160
Well, tiramisu is a really
popular, sweet, creamy,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.390
and coffee flavor dessert,

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.730
which typically starts
with a layer of coffee

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.470
or espresso soaked lady fingers.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.310
And then on top of that

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.950
a layer of lightly sweetened
whipped cream and mascarpone,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.660
and then dusted with cocoa powder.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.000
Traditionally, we soak
lady fingers in coffee,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.770
but obviously that's
gonna be soggy, right?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.090
So I saw this thing on TikTok

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.350
where you can make a rice chip,

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.600
but instead of using water,
we're gonna use coffee

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.790
to have that flavor and keep that crisp.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.900
All right, so first things first,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.450
we're gonna rinse the rice to
get rid of that extra starch.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.800
Now our rice is washed.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.850
Now we're gonna add the coffee

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.050
and we're gonna bring this to a boil,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.630
and once it boils, we're gonna
simmer it for about an hour.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.400
You just really wanna overcook the rice,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.890
so it's easier to blend later
'cause we're gonna make paste.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:44.910
Okay, it's been an hour.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.150
And as you can see the coffee
has been absorbed by the rice,

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.670
and it's very overcooked, it's very soft.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.250
Usually you really don't
wanna serve rice like this,

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.470
but for this recipe,
this is what you need.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.750
We're gonna blend the overcooked rice

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.370
even though it looks really weird,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:00.840
but you gotta trust the process.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.486
You're just looking for a
really smooth consistency.

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:04.620
(blender whirring)

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.670
Okay, this looks great.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.530
It looks like a very loose peanut butter

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.700
but that's what you're looking for

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.080
'cause we're gonna spread it nice and thin

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:10.913
on a baking sheet.

00:02:10.913 --> 00:02:13.560
Now I'm going to spread
the paste that we made,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.650
the coffee rice paste, so we
can put it in the dehydrator.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.840
I hope the coffee flavor is strong enough

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.340
so we can taste it in the dish.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.140
But I also hope it's not too strong

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.940
that it overpowers the entire thing.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.890
You want it thin 'cause if it's too thick

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.600
it takes too long to dehydrate.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.580
So just try your best to keep it even.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.080
Okay, so now I'm gonna scoop the rest

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:31.920
and we'll be right back.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.600
And now we're gonna dehydrate it

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.820
with our handy dandy dehydrator.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.760
A dehydrator is pretty much
just like a gentle oven.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.680
It goes slower than a usual oven

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.780
and it's more precise and accurate.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.730
If you don't have this,
you can also use an oven.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.610
Okay, so we are going to
dehydrate this for about two hours

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.380
or until it looks like a fruit roll up.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.990
Now we're making the tiramisu cream.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.610
I'm gonna keep it traditional.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.980
We have egg yolks and a double boiler.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.030
You're gonna add your sugar.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.650
You want to gently cook the eggs

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.510
without turning into scrambled eggs.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.510
And you also don't wanna
serve raw eggs, you know?

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.210
Now that it's pale and triple in volume,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.490
I can tell that it's ready to go.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.890
And now we're going to
add our mascarpone cream.

00:03:13.386 --> 00:03:14.578
(staff laughing)

00:03:14.578 --> 00:03:15.840
(beeps)
(upbeat music)

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.460
So we're just folding the mascarpone

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.311
in the egg yolk mixture.

00:03:19.311 --> 00:03:20.700
(upbeat music)

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.710
Our mascarpone in egg yolk mixture

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.720
is nice, smooth, and silky.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.430
It's time to make the whipped cream.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.560
I asked for an electric mixer

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.090
'cause I got really
tired from the last one.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.000
So you wanna mix just the cream
until it's nice and frothy.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.020
And then we add the sugar.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.030
You wanna whip into like a soft peak

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.140
'cause we're gonna fold it

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.690
in the mascarpone egg yolk mixture.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.300
So we're gonna add a third of
the whipped cream at a time,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.970
so we can slowly get that
texture that we want.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.040
We want it nice and airy,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.470
and just incorporate
as much air as you can.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.400
All right, I'm happy with this.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.270
We're gonna let this chill,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.750
and let's move on to my
favorite part, which is frying.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.220
Okay, so this is the coffee rice leather

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.330
that we've been working on.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.163
This came out perfect.

00:03:58.163 --> 00:03:59.850
So it's still flexible.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.110
It's like a fruit roll up,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.720
and it should puff up when we fry it.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.490
So we're gonna cut this in smaller pieces,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.100
so when we fry it we
can stack it at the end.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.120
Doesn't have to be perfect

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.040
'cause when we fry it
it's just gonna curl up.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:11.873
Moment of truth.

00:04:11.873 --> 00:04:13.410
If I did this correctly,

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.840
it should puff up the
moment it touches the oil.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.735
All right, here we go.

00:04:17.735 --> 00:04:19.950
(oil sizzling)

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.513
I'm hearing the right sounds.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.713
Now we have our coffee rice chip.

00:04:27.435 --> 00:04:28.860
Ta-da.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.940
I'm pretty happy with that.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.790
Okay, so this is exactly
what I was looking for.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.023
Nice and crispy.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.420
Thank god it worked.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.830
Now it's time for assembly

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.170
which is my favorite part
'cause it's the easiest,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.340
